From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:31:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the errno the title reads is Horizon's, and a C call
 answers -errno

The socket C entries answer the title's own C library (the SDK's nnmusl), whose layer takes a
syscall's answer as -errno and turns it into errno = -r. Answering -1 there makes the title read
errno 1 (EPERM) whatever went wrong, so a non-blocking read that merely found nothing yet looks
like a hard failure to its TLS stack: log A233283B shows the NPLN client dropping the connection
right after writing its HTTP/2 preface, with no GetLastErrno call at all.

The numbers the title compares are Horizon's, which are the Linux ones (Ryujinx Nextendo,
LinuxError.cs: EAGAIN 11, EINPROGRESS 115, ECONNRESET 104, ENOTCONN 107, ENETDOWN 100), not this
host's Darwin ones (35, 36, 54, 57, 50). GetLastErrno and GetLastError hand back the same
numbering, and SetLastErrno takes it back.

poll reports a closed peer as a hang-up whatever the caller asked for, as poll(2) says: the
client's transport polls with events 0 after its write, and an answer of nothing there leaves it
waiting for a reply no one will send.

A stream socket's close is traced, and a failed receive names both numbers, because a client that
gives up on a connection and a server that dropped it read the same in a log.

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: The title's socket calls go through its own C library (the SDK's nnmusl), whose layer takes a syscall's
  answer as -errno, and every failure was answered -1: the title then read errno 1 (EPERM) whatever went wrong,
  so a non-blocking read that had merely found nothing yet looked like a hard failure to its TLS stack. Log
  A233283B (patch 0144 installed) shows the handshake completing, the server's HTTP/2 SETTINGS arriving, the
  client writing its 128-byte preface and closing at once, with not one GetLastErrno in the whole log: the error
  it decided on never came from us. The numbers were wrong too — Horizon's socket errors are the Linux ones
  (Ryujinx Nextendo, LinuxError.cs), so EAGAIN is 11, not this host's 35. And the client's transport polls with
  events 0 after its write, where poll(2) still reports a hang-up and ours answered nothing.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

